13
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.410
and yes, discomfort.

14
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.730
But discomfort is, again,

15
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.930
the price of admission to a meaningful life.

16
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.270
Our minds are so powerful,

17
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.540
so even just like changing the storyline

18
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.530
makes it a whole different game.

19
00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.200
Just try to find a way to get 1% better each day.

20
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.130
Interrupting the patterns of thought

21
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.337
that are holding you back.

22
00:00:47.337 --> 00:00:49.970
It is the only way you are gonna change.

23
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.490
Mindset is the single most important thing

24
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.803
that anyone can take out of this conversation.

25
00:01:00.424 --> 00:01:02.270
Hey, everybody, welcome to the podcast.

26
00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.910
Today we're gonna go deep on mindset,

27
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:05.743
and we're gonna do it

28
00:01:05.743 --> 00:01:08.640
through the lens of experience and wisdom

29
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.550
by dint of some of the most successful,

30
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.883
impressive and mentally tough people on the planet.

31
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.370
By way of background, for the last nine years,

32
00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.720
I have put everything into this podcast

33
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.510
and through conversation,

34
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.230
we have compiled this really powerful arsenal

35
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:29.940
of potent life-altering ideas, perspectives and tools

36
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.404
on how to cultivate, embrace and apply

37
00:01:33.404 --> 00:01:37.510
a new and more personally meaningful approach to life.

38
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.880
And so for the fourth time,

39
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.020
today we present you

40
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.170
with this very special Masterclass episode

41
00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:49.020
that anthologizes the best wisdom shared over the years

42
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.180
regarding habit change science,

43
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.910
how to overcome obstacles and foster a mindset

44
00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.610
that will, ultimately, improve every aspect of your life.

45
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.430
Because in the words of my friend, Mel Robbins,

46
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.130
mindset changes you.

47
00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.050
So this is a powerful and dare I say,

48
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.250
potentially life-changing collection of wisdom,

49
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.380
and I can't wait for you to hear it.

50
00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.890
Starting with an extraordinary athlete,

51
00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:22.700
a true master of mindset, Leia Goldstein.

52
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.570
Leia Goldstein is, undeniably,

53
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.600
one of the toughest people I've ever met.

54
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.640
At just 17, she donned the Bantamweight

55
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.400
later joining the Israeli Defense Force

56
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.480
and becoming a Krav Maga specialist

57
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.430
and an undercover special forces intelligence officer.

58
00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.500
She then embarked on a professional cycling career,

59
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.820
but after being told she might not ever walk again,

60
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.930
following a devastating crash,

61
00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.290
Leia leaned into her superpower,

62
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.500
her mindset,

63
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.530
to aid her recovery and launch a brand new chapter

64
00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.790
in her extraordinary life.

65
00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.790
In 2021, at age 52 and entirely plant-based I might add,

66
00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.110
Leia became the very first woman in the 39-year history

67
00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.090
of RAAM, the 3000-mile Race Across America,

68
00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:16.030
to beat everyone, including all the men,

69
00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.040
and outright win the Solo division.

70
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.700
Here's a glimpse into the power of mindset

71
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.260
when you've been told your physical prowess is over,

72
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.490
that healing will be lifelong

73
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.700
and that achievements will be something of your past

74
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.910
and not your future.

75
00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.249
When I reached the peak,

76
00:03:34.249 --> 00:03:37.470
at that point where opportunities were handed to me,

77
00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.790
I had the mother of all crashes in Cascade.

78
00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.240
You start descending and on those bikes,

79
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.733
you can go very fast, almost up to, what is it,

80
00:03:43.733 --> 00:03:46.650
a 100 kilometers an hour or 80 miles an hour.

81
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:47.980
And as we're descending,

82
00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:49.630
other riders are starting to come up

83
00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.850
and I'm seeing like 85, 86,

84
00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.840
and then there's a rider kind of coming on my left-hand side

85
00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.700
and you know, there's a center line rule,

86
00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.030
and she kinda leans into me at 80 kilometers an hour.

87
00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.280
I land on my face.

88
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:03.113
It was a nightmare.

89
00:04:03.113 --> 00:04:04.790
I mean, I shouldn't be sitting here today.

90
00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.000
Right.

91
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.980
So I believe, correct me if I'm wrong,

92
00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.910
VeloNews dubbed this crash

93
00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.740
the most epic crash of all time?

94
00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.130
At that time, correct, yes.

95
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.360
And you were told that you might not walk properly again?

96
00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.193
Correct.

97
00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.420
Both my ischium was shattered.

98
00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.330
My hips were broken.

99
00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.690
My arms...

100
00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:26.190
I mean, fingers and toes busted, right?

101
00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.023
I was...

102
00:04:27.023 --> 00:04:27.856
It's like taking a pretzel,

103
00:04:27.856 --> 00:04:29.670
and basically stepping on it, right?

104
00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.080
I can't even explain it to you.

105
00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.360
The worst pain I'd ever been through.

106
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.510
Everything hurt, all the time.

107
00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.870
Breathing, blinking, people opening and closing the door,

108
00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.170
the wind from that, right?

109
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.840
It was a tough go.
Wow.

110
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.980
How long were you in the hospital?

111
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.180
Well, they couldn't move me out of the United States

112
00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.080
for, I think, four weeks.

113
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.900
And then, they transferred me,

114
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.820
'cause I had to be airlifted

115
00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.280
from the crash to St. Charles, in Bend, Oregon.

116
00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.000
And then, my sister came to pick me up,

117
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.740
'cause I couldn't...

118
00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:59.420
It was gonna take a big process to drive me back to Canada.

119
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:00.610
It was also getting expensive,

120
00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.130
'cause medical is expensive here, right? (laughs)

121
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.360
Sure.

122
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.193
Yeah.

123
00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.080
Okay, so months in the hospital?

124
00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.640
Yes
But you have the gift

125
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.670
of these doctors telling you you're not gonna walk again,

126
00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.313
let alone get on a bike and ever race again, right?

127
00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.763
So what do you do with that information?

128
00:05:18.122 --> 00:05:19.760
You know what I said to them in my head?

129
00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.380
I won't say it on air.
Right. (laughs)

130
00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:22.213
Gosh, no!

131
00:05:22.213 --> 00:05:24.380
I mean, how do you begin the rehab process?

132
00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.650
Well, listen, the only thing I could do,

133
00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:27.810
was contract my abs.

134
00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.600
That was the only physical thing I could do, right?

135
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.667
But I basically made a promise to myself and I said,

136
00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:34.364
"I don't care how long,

137
00:05:34.364 --> 00:05:35.860
or it takes or the kinda pain I'm gonna go through.

138
00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.020
I'm gonna get back on the bike,

139
00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.480
I'm gonna race again and I'm gonna come back even stronger

140
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.256
than I did before all this happened, right?

141
00:05:42.256 --> 00:05:43.780
And I think, you know...

142
00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.900
It happens to a lot of people,

143
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:46.850
when you're faced with these overwhelming situations,

144
00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:49.850
you don't know what to do, so you kinda buckle, right?

145
00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:51.700
So I knew I had a lot of work cut out for me,

146
00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.710
but I think everything is upstairs.

147
00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:55.530
It's what you believe in and what you make your body believe

148
00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.380
that you can do, right?

149
00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:58.213
Because honestly,

150
00:05:58.213 --> 00:06:00.640
if I believed everything that I was told,

151
00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:01.860
even growing up, then I wouldn't...

152
00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.220
Again, I wouldn't be sitting here today.

153
00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.053
Right?

154
00:06:04.053 --> 00:06:08.120
But you know, we all have our breaking points too.

155
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.930
But your ability to kind of compartmentalize all of that,

156
00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:12.987
and just focus on,

157
00:06:12.987 --> 00:06:14.803
"Okay, I can do this"

158
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.860
How much of your military training comes into play,

159
00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:22.060
in terms of having the mental rigor

160
00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:27.060
to just block out the fear and focus on the task at hand?

161
00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.300
I think the military has a lot to do with it,

162
00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.220
because it was part of their job,

163
00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.450
even during my military training,

164
00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.850
to crack us mentally as best as they can, right?

165
00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.680
'Cause mental strength is much more powerful

166
00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:39.590
than physical strength.
Mm-hmm.

167
00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:40.440
We all know that.

168
00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.420
But for me, at that point going back to the hospital,

169
00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.400
I mean, how can they say something like that?

170
00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.930
I haven't even started my rehab.

171
00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:48.763
You know what I mean?

172
00:06:48.763 --> 00:06:51.697
Just to make that kind of prediction that fast, right?

173
00:06:51.697 --> 00:06:52.530
And I think that's the thing,

174
00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:57.530
is that we're so quick to take the easy way out, right?

175
00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.750
Without being more optimistic of the things

176
00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:03.070
that we possibly can do, and the possibilities

177
00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:06.400
of just going after the things that you believe in, right?

178
00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:07.270
'Cause sometimes,

179
00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:08.850
you're not gonna get support from other people,

180
00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.447
You're gonna have to believe in yourself, right?

181
00:07:10.447 --> 00:07:11.280
And that's the thing,

182
00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.410
is we're so influenced by other sources.

183
00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:14.570
But for me, I never was,

184
00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.594
never has been and never will be.

185
00:07:16.594 --> 00:07:18.180
What I wanna do and what I'm determined, to do,

186
00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:19.370
I'll make it happen.

187
00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:21.400
I'll find a way to make it happen.

188
00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:22.233
Right.

189
00:07:22.233 --> 00:07:24.290
So make it happen you did.

190
00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.670
How long was the rehabilitation

191
00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.600
before you could get back on a bike?

192
00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:32.263
I was back on my bike, race-ready, in less than a season.

193
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:34.673
So like-
Eight months.

194
00:07:34.673 --> 00:07:36.100
Eight months... Wow.

195
00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.910
But every day, I was better than the day before

196
00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.650
and just staying positive.

197
00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.010
And honestly, it sorta got...

198
00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.710
Being in that positive mode,

199
00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.840
I could feel things starting to bind.

200
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.620
'Cause when I went to get re x-rayed at the General Hospital

201
00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.950
in Vancouver a couple of months later,

202
00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.814
they were floored at how fast I was recovering.

203
00:07:53.814 --> 00:07:54.750
Mm.

204
00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:58.220
And you attribute that to just being in motion and-

205
00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:00.450
I think motion and mentally, you know what I mean?

206
00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.760
And just determined that I am gonna get out of this.

207
00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.600
Yeah.

208
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.040
So from the wheelchair then, how do you...

209
00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.260
I mean, are you walking with a limp?

210
00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:12.010
Oh yeah, no, it goes from wheelchair to a one crutch.

211
00:08:12.871 --> 00:08:13.704
You know what I mean, popping away.

212
00:08:13.704 --> 00:08:15.910
And then, I mean, even...

213
00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:19.380
It was even a wheelchair to wheeling to my trainer,

214
00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.780
which was on a bike, whatever.

215
00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.040
And it took me like 40 minutes

216
00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.120
just to get from the wheelchair,

217
00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.610
just to sit and be comfortable on the bike, because...

218
00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.410
It was the flashbacks.
(air whooshing)

219
00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.280
Despite the age-old adage,

220
00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:32.900
you can't teach an old dog new tricks,

221
00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.230
what if I told you

222
00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:37.450
that you actually do have the power to change your brain

223
00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:42.150
and reprogram your perception, irrespective of age?

224
00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:46.700
Meet neuroplasticity overlord, Dr. Andrew Huberman.

225
00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.050
A neuroscientist and tenured professor

226
00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.810
in the Department of Neurobiology

227
00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:53.100
at Stanford University School of Medicine,

228
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.660
Andrew specializes in the brain's inherent ability

229
00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.510
to modify itself based on experience,

230
00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:04.630
and the many ways we can advantageously leverage

231
00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:06.110
this process.

232
00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:09.510
Here's a quick lesson on how to shift your perspective,

233
00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:12.273
and sharpen your mindset through behavior.

234
00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.480
It's incredible, 'cause if you think about sensation,

235
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.190
perception, feeling, thought and behavior,

236
00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.500
actually the way to control our nervous system

237
00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.620
and feel the way we wanna feel

238
00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.640
is to run that backwards.

239
00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.130
Behavior, thoughts.

240
00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.760
So if you change your behavior, then, generally,

241
00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.210
your thoughts, your feelings and your perceptions change.

242
00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:33.900
And everyone tries to come at it from the other end,

243
00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.040 line:15%
but he's figured out,

244
00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.970 line:15%
through whatever process led him there

245
00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.140 line:15%
and incredible life circumstances,

246
00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.160
how to run it in this direction of behavior first.

247
00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:43.560
Yeah.
And I really think

248
00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.130
that if neuroscience has anything to offer,

249
00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:47.890
it's some understanding

250
00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.450
of what the underlying chemicals and neural circuits are,

251
00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:53.650
but the sooner that the human animal,

252
00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:55.680
the human species, can start to understand

253
00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.040
that our feelings and our thoughts and our memories and...

254
00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:00.740
All that is very complicated,

255
00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.980
but that when behaviors are very concrete

256
00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:08.450
and they are the control panel for the rest of it...

257
00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:09.810
I don't wanna relegate feelings.

258
00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.520
Feelings are extremely important.

259
00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.110
I don't wanna relegate perception.

260
00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.810
They're extremely important.

261
00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.920
But when it comes to wanting to shift the way

262
00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:18.820
that you function to get better

263
00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:21.690
or to perform better or to show up better

264
00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:25.120
or to move away from things like addictive behaviors,

265
00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.030
it's absolutely foolish for any of us,

266
00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.020
me included, to think that we can do that

267
00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:31.560
by changing our thoughts first.

268
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.620
It's behavior first,

269
00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.360
thoughts, feelings and perceptions follow.

270
00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.293
Mood follows action?
Mood follows action-

271
00:10:38.293 --> 00:10:42.410
This has been my mantra forever, and I swear by it.

272
00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.560
And David's example illustrates that,

273
00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:46.540
that act first...

274
00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:50.160
He's developed so much neuroplasticity

275
00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.990
that it's reflexive for him

276
00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.190
to just move towards the hard thing

277
00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:57.830
or the challenge or the discomfort, right?

278
00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.240
And now, the science establishes

279
00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.980
that this is indeed the case.

280
00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.720
And yet, our programming, our default hardwiring,

281
00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.980
is to put us in this place

282
00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.160
where we wanna ruminate on all this stuff,

283
00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.220
and wait until we feel like doing something

284
00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.970
before we do it or check our motivations for it.

285
00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:19.960
But anytime I'm in a funk, or I wanna change my state,

286
00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:21.160
I have to move forward.

287
00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.650
I have to do something with my physical body

288
00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:25.420
in order to shake things up

289
00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:30.360
and rearrange whatever's going on mentally, so...

290
00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.370
And it works every time.

291
00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.870
It works every time because the brain circuits,

292
00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:37.140
meaning sets of connections and chemicals,

293
00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:39.010
they're there from birth, they're there your whole life,

294
00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:40.720
and they were designed for that.

295
00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.010
So in 2018, a graduate student in my lab

296
00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:44.100
published a paper in Nature,

297
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.313
showing that in the face of a physical threat,

298
00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:49.220
there are three options.

299
00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:51.080
You can, obviously, freeze,

300
00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:53.850
you can retreat or you can move forward.

301
00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:57.420
And the moving forward response actually triggers activation

302
00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.270
of a connection in the brain,

303
00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.180
to the dopamine circuitry of the brain,

304
00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:02.710
and makes it more likely

305
00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:05.820
that you're gonna be able to move forward in the future.

306
00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:07.200
Now, what was interesting to us,

307
00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.760
was that not only is forward action rewarded

308
00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.450
at a neurochemical level,

309
00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.010
which then sets you up for more forward action,

310
00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.360
but the highest level of agitation and stress

311
00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.000
was associated with moving forward.

312
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.760
We always think, "Well, if I just calm myself enough,

313
00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:24.350
I'll be able to move forward."
Right.

314
00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.371
But it's the exact opposite.
(Rich laughing)

315
00:12:26.371 --> 00:12:29.660
And so people who are paralyzed in fear,

316
00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:32.130
or that have a hard time initiating,

317
00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.210
sometimes the key is to raise the level

318
00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:35.300
of stress and agitation.

319
00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.823
This is why deadlines are so effective.

320
00:12:36.823 --> 00:12:38.830
This is why fear is so effective.

321
00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:42.780
This is why that deer gets up out of its nice little den

322
00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:43.700
and starts to move,

323
00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.340
because it feels a certain level of agitation.

324
00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:47.810
If that agitation isn't high enough,

325
00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:49.260
we will not move forward.

326
00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.520
And so, especially in the US,

327
00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.940
we have a culture in which these ideas around stress

328
00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:57.400
are that it's terrible for us,

329
00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.430
when in fact, stress is designed to move us forward

330
00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:02.630
towards these action steps that are rewarded,

331
00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:05.570
which then move us forward and so on.

332
00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:10.360
So what is the process of, of combating that monkey mind

333
00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:14.420
that is running whatever narrative that's keeping you stuck.

334
00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:15.547
Like it's easy to say,

335
00:13:15.547 --> 00:13:17.387
"Just move, you gotta take the action."

336
00:13:17.387 --> 00:13:19.210
But a lot of people still,

337
00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:23.290
despite understanding that, intellectualizing that,

338
00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:28.290
are unable to basically act as if.

339
00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:32.030
Some people are just hypo-aroused.

340
00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:34.200
They're just not motivated enough.

341
00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.530
And those people would benefit greatly

342
00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:40.330
from cultivating practices like super-oxygenated breathing.

343
00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:41.670
So this is something along the lines

344
00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.990
of tummo-type breathing, so rapid.

345
00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:44.920
And we look at this in the lab...

346
00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:46.950
We're actually running a human study on this now.

347
00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:49.600
So 25 or 30 deep breaths through the nose

348
00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.380
and out through the mouth,

349
00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:53.850
then exhaling the breath and holding,

350
00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:56.600
learning to how to self-generate adrenaline.

351
00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:57.840
That's what you're doing when you're that-

352
00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.570
Some version of the Wim Hof technique or-

353
00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:01.600
That's what that is.

354
00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.010
Brian Mackenzie talks about it-

355
00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.970
Some people are so agitated, the monkey mind,

356
00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:06.807
you've got too many things going on,

357
00:14:06.807 --> 00:14:07.970
and they're thinking, "Okay..."

358
00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:09.110
They're trying to sit down and write.

359
00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:09.950
I suffer from this.

360
00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:10.877
And I'm feeling like,

361
00:14:10.877 --> 00:14:13.410
"Wait, I've also got this person I need to connect with,

362
00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.360
and I'm kinda being drawn off course,

363
00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.720
by not being able to put the blinders on.

364
00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:19.290
For people that have that issue,

365
00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.270
I think learning how to calm the nervous system

366
00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:23.940
is very powerful.

367
00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:27.070
And a physiological sigh is two inhales,

368
00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:28.760
followed by an extended exhale.

369
00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.980
So it's like...
(Andrew sniffing)

370
00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:31.813
(Andrew exhaling )

371
00:14:31.813 --> 00:14:33.430
It's not just a deep breath.

372
00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:36.650
It's two inhales followed by an exhale, okay?

373
00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:40.100
And then, what that does, and this has been shown

374
00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:42.970
several times now in humans and other species, as well,

375
00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:47.210
is it dilates the sacs of the lungs and that second inhale,

376
00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.170
dilates them a little bit more.

377
00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.070
And it pulls a little bit of carbon dioxide

378
00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:51.903
out of the bloodstream,

379
00:14:51.903 --> 00:14:52.780
so that when we exhale,

380
00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.220
we offload the maximum amount of carbon dioxide,

381
00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:57.070
and it perfectly adjusts the ratio

382
00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.980
of carbon dioxide and oxygen in the bloodstream and lungs.

383
00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:01.480
And sometimes, it only takes one

384
00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:02.750
of these double inhale exhales.

385
00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:05.900
Sometimes somebody needs to do two or three.

386
00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:07.570
But that's the fastest way

387
00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:09.720
to bring the autonomic nervous system down.

388
00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.330
A lot of people need such a tool,

389
00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.960
because I think we talk a lot about meditation

390
00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.160
and tools for calm,

391
00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.990
and I can go to Esalen for a weekend and get a massage,

392
00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:20.823
I'm gonna feel very good.

393
00:15:20.823 --> 00:15:22.460
But then, when I'm thrown back in real life,

394
00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.563
I need something that's gonna work in real-time.

395
00:15:24.563 --> 00:15:26.750
What I call a real-time tool.

396
00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.450
Everybody should have a kit of tools,

397
00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:31.730
that they can use to bring themselves down

398
00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:33.270
and ramp themselves up.

399
00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:34.830
And then, what people don't realize

400
00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:38.480
is that mental focus follows visual focus.

401
00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.030
Now, in blind people, it's slightly different.

402
00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:41.520
It follows auditory focus.

403
00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.770
But in most people, your visual focus,

404
00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:48.800
as you bring that into really sharp relief,

405
00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.490
that image of your book, and you stare at it,

406
00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:51.960
you're gonna feel some agitation,

407
00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:53.500
and your mind's gonna be jumping all over the place.

408
00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.290
But if you wait just a couple minutes,

409
00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:57.250
the rest of the world will disappear.

410
00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:59.420
I think this is sort of the flow state

411
00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:00.720
people are looking for.

412
00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:02.140
But remember, the gate of entry

413
00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:05.140
is one of you have to wade through some sewage

414
00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:06.267
before you can swim in clear water, right?

415
00:16:06.267 --> 00:16:08.400
That's the way I always think about it.

416
00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.570
But the visual focus is what brings the rest of the brain

417
00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:14.750
into cognitive focus.

418
00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:18.130
And people in the martial arts understand this.

419
00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.780
You've probably experienced this running,

420
00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:21.210
when you're feeling exhausted

421
00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:24.350
and you can just concentrate on one milestone,

422
00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:25.640
and get there.

423
00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:28.300
You can almost bring that into...

424
00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:29.850
What you're doing is you're linking that

425
00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.180
to the dopamine circuitry.

426
00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:33.750
You're saying that thing is the milestone.

427
00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:35.040
Not winning the race,

428
00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:38.650
not some other thing outside this immediate environment.

429
00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:39.483
That thing.

430
00:16:39.483 --> 00:16:41.170
And when you're able to start capturing

431
00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:44.140
these peripheral circuits, meaning the body, the diaphragm,

432
00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:45.620
the visual system,

433
00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:49.120
then you start getting past this whole idea of mindsets,

434
00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:52.084
and it really becomes about the body setting the mind.

435
00:16:52.084 --> 00:16:53.407
And this is where, I think, when you say,

436
00:16:53.407 --> 00:16:55.660
"Action leads the rest-"
Mm-hmm.

437
00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:56.493
Right?

438
00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:00.010
What you're saying is grounded

439
00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.963
in real neurobiological data.

440
00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:06.590
Next up, we're gonna hear from the humble master

441
00:17:06.590 --> 00:17:09.540
of grit and boundary-busting physical prowess,

442
00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:11.750
Courtney Dauwalter.

443
00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:14.410
Arguably the world's best ultrarunner,

444
00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.120
Courtney's recipe for success

445
00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:18.930
isn't about cutting edge training plans,

446
00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.680
coaches or carefully-honed nutrition.

447
00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:22.670
In fact,

448
00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:25.140
she actually abides by none of these.

449
00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:29.030
For her, it's instead found in seeking out and celebrating

450
00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.210
what she calls a pain cave,

451
00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.230
that deep place of physical discomfort

452
00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:37.150
that most go to great lengths to avoid.

453
00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:37.983
In this clip,

454
00:17:37.983 --> 00:17:38.950
Courtney shares a few

455
00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:41.080
of her mindset techniques and tactics

456
00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:44.130
that have propelled her superhuman feats of endurance,

457
00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:47.700
and exactly what it means to carve a path

458
00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.533
through your own pain cave.

459
00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:53.570
Tell me a little bit more about what that is,

460
00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:55.920
when you reach that point or that limit,

461
00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.320
or that place where you feel like

462
00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:00.683
you can't put one foot in front of the other.

463
00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:06.373
What is the lesson that you find for yourself in that?

464
00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:11.050
So I call it the pain cave, that place.

465
00:18:11.050 --> 00:18:16.050
And I guess, probably four or five years ago,

466
00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:18.290
I viewed the pain cave

467
00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:21.270
as this place that you should try to put off

468
00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:23.370
as long as possible in a race,

469
00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:27.010
like make your pain cave be as far away from you as you can.

470
00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:29.580
And if you arrive to it,

471
00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:31.030
then you just sit in it,

472
00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:33.403
and you try and survive the pain cave.

473
00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:36.340
But in the past couple years...

474
00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.090
I mean, it's just a mindset, right?

475
00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.680
It's like all in our heads, this thing.

476
00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:42.670
And in the past couple years,

477
00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:45.500
it's been the place I want to get to.

478
00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:48.330
So changing it to a place

479
00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:50.500
where I get to celebrate that I made it there,

480
00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:53.910
and then, that's where the work actually happens.

481
00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:57.480
So making the pain cave bigger is how I view it,

482
00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:01.080
instead of pushing the pain cave away.

483
00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:04.320
And I think, I mean, our minds are so powerful,

484
00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:07.240
so even just like changing the storyline

485
00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:09.582
makes it a whole different game.

486
00:19:09.582 --> 00:19:10.415
Right.

487
00:19:10.415 --> 00:19:12.480
So what is the story that you...

488
00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:14.470
What is the script that you flip

489
00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:18.500
when you're in that headspace and it's getting really hard.

490
00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:21.670
Yeah, it's like, "Perfect, this is what we wanted.

491
00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:24.280
Now, we get to actually do the hard work

492
00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:26.780
of making the cave bigger."

493
00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:29.090
And so it's like picturing a chisel,

494
00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:32.410
and just making tunnels in my pain cave

495
00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:33.960
in my brain. (laughs)
Right.

496
00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:36.579
You actually visualize that?

497
00:19:36.579 --> 00:19:37.412
Yeah.
Yeah?

498
00:19:37.412 --> 00:19:39.278
I'm super visual, so...
Yeah.

499
00:19:39.278 --> 00:19:40.111
Yeah.

500
00:19:40.111 --> 00:19:40.944
I like that.

501
00:19:40.944 --> 00:19:42.710
It makes it very visceral and real,

502
00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:43.543
if you can...

503
00:19:43.543 --> 00:19:46.170
It's not just a mantra and mantras are great.

504
00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:47.900
I'm sure you have mantras.

505
00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:51.390
But actually creating that three-dimensional image

506
00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:53.050
in your mind.

507
00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:53.990
Yeah.

508
00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:54.823
Yeah.

509
00:19:54.823 --> 00:19:56.670
And it's just telling myself a different story

510
00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:59.900
about that place, where it hurts so bad,

511
00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:02.290
where before it was like surviving it,

512
00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:03.877
and now it's like,

513
00:20:03.877 --> 00:20:08.340
"This is so cool we made it here, and now, we work."

514
00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:09.680
Yeah.

515
00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:12.350
It's funny, I've been doing this podcast thing

516
00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:14.360
for a couple years at this point,

517
00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:16.860
and I've had a lot of people on who have done hard things

518
00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:19.510
and I have psychologists and psychiatrists

519
00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:21.390
and mindset experts,

520
00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:23.367
and everybody kinda comes to the table and they're like,

521
00:20:23.367 --> 00:20:24.480
"This is how you do it."

522
00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:26.299
And it's like, "Step one, two, three.

523
00:20:26.299 --> 00:20:28.080
And like, "When your mind does this..."

524
00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.057
And you're just like, "Well, I just..."

525
00:20:30.057 --> 00:20:31.540
(Courtney laughing)

526
00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:33.790
It's very refreshing,

527
00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:35.970
because what it does

528
00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:40.480
is it dispels this myth that it has to be complicated,

529
00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:43.060
or that there is a right or a wrong way.

530
00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:46.040
You're just embracing life in all its colors,

531
00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:48.870
and have figured out this thing that works for you.

532
00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.457
But it's welcoming to people ,

533
00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.117
because you're saying like,

534
00:20:53.117 --> 00:20:54.400
"Look, I'm doing this.

535
00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:55.610
You can do this too."

536
00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:56.443
There's a...,

537
00:20:58.496 --> 00:20:59.596
What's the right word?

538
00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:04.190
Welcoming, to repeat myself, I think is what it is.

539
00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:06.320
Like you're creating space for other people

540
00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:09.500
to see greater possibility in themselves

541
00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.857
because of that relatability.

542
00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.370
Well, thank you.
Yeah.

543
00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:15.203
That's very kind.
Yeah.

544
00:21:15.203 --> 00:21:16.100
No, I think it's powerful.

545
00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:17.283
It's really powerful.

546
00:21:19.495 --> 00:21:24.110
In the equation of mind versus body,

547
00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:25.650
how do you think about that?

548
00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:29.793
How much of it is physical prowess versus mental grit?

549
00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:34.720
It's both, for sure, and I think in an ultra,

550
00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:37.100
it trades back and forth between the two.

551
00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:39.230
So maybe for a while,

552
00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:43.690
your physical has to pull more of the weight because it can,

553
00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:45.360
and then, if that's giving out,

554
00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:48.090
maybe the mental takes over for a while.

555
00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:52.010
So I think they tag each other out back and forth

556
00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:54.470
where you need 'em for sure.

557
00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:58.080
Like physically, it's hard to run this far,

558
00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:03.070
but mentally you can move your feet much farther

559
00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:04.230
than you think.
Right.

560
00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:06.920
Yeah, ultimately, I think,

561
00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:10.210
the differentiator is in the mental game

562
00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:13.060
because everybody, especially at the elite level,

563
00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:14.690
is training really hard.

564
00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:17.080
And there's only so much training that you can do

565
00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:19.440
before you get injured or you overtrain, right?

566
00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.640
So when you tow the line at the starting line,

567
00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:22.473
you can be assured

568
00:22:22.473 --> 00:22:26.240
that everybody who's a threat to your dominance

569
00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:28.320
has put in the work that you've put in, right?

570
00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.530
So the person who's gonna win,

571
00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:34.010
it's gonna come down to who's gonna crack mentally,

572
00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:36.300
when the tough gets going.

573
00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:37.560
Yeah.

574
00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:42.300
Yeah, and who can problem-solve efficiently,

575
00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:44.740
or not let problems that come up

576
00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:47.000
ruffle their feathers too much.

577
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:48.680
I think that's huge in ultras.

578
00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:50.470
Yeah, just being able to maintain

579
00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:52.430
that positive disposition,

580
00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:54.270
rather than, "Oh no, this is terrible"

581
00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.416
think, "Awesome, this is what it's about-"

582
00:22:56.416 --> 00:22:59.670
Yeah, exactly.
(Courtney and Rich laughing)

583
00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.390
How do you keep a smile on your face and tell jokes,

584
00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:04.869
and do all that stuff when you're so freaking exhausted.

585
00:23:04.869 --> 00:23:07.690
(Courtney laughing)

586
00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:09.400
Jokes help everything.
Yeah.

587
00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:10.810
Yeah. (laughs)

588
00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:12.550
That's part of the strategy.

589
00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:13.383
Yeah.
Sneaky.

590
00:23:13.383 --> 00:23:15.090
Yeah. (laughs)

591
00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:16.398
(air whooshing)

592
00:23:16.398 --> 00:23:20.481
(upbeat percussive tribal music)

593
00:23:50.534 --> 00:23:52.400
(air whooshing)

594
00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:54.360
Mastering mindset begins

595
00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:58.100
with expanding your sense of personal possibility.

596
00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:02.560
That is the hidden key that unlocks reservoirs of potential

597
00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:04.940
and ultimately, sets you on the path

598
00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:08.870
to becoming the best version of who you are.

599
00:24:08.870 --> 00:24:12.320
I can think of no better exemplar of this truth

600
00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:15.620
than the mighty one himself, David Goggins.

601
00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:18.800
Often referred to as the toughest athlete on the planet,

602
00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:22.930
David is the only member of the US Armed Forces

603
00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:27.740
to have completed SEAL training, including three Hell Weeks,

604
00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:30.930
in addition to the US Army Ranger School

605
00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:34.640
and Air Force Tactical Air Controller Training.

606
00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:36.820
His remarkable feats of strength

607
00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:39.510
include finishing near the top at dozens

608
00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.720
of the world's most grueling endurance races,

609
00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:45.790
including the HURT100, the Leadville 100, the Moab 240,

610
00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:48.270
Western States and many more,

611
00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:50.920
but perhaps David's greatest accomplishment

612
00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:52.320
is that throughout his life,

613
00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.940
he has faced and overcome a succession

614
00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:57.490
of just seemingly insurmountable obstacles

615
00:24:57.490 --> 00:25:00.180
to become the man he is today.

616
00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.860
Obstacles like asthma, sickle cell anemia,

617
00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:04.740
psychological and physical abuse,

618
00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:08.260
obesity and even a congenital heart defect

619
00:25:08.260 --> 00:25:11.200
that often left him competing and winning

620
00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:14.920
on a mere fraction of his actual physical capabilities.

621
00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.030
All of which is, of course,

622
00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:19.337
chronicled in his fascinating memoir, "Can't Hurt Me".

623
00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:21.849
Here is David Goggins.

624
00:25:21.849 --> 00:25:23.820
(air whooshing)

625
00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:25.340
So think about this.

626
00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:28.243
I put everything on David Goggins to be a Navy SEAL.

627
00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:33.430
It's like going to the crap table with your last $1,000,

628
00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:34.350
and you say," You know what?

629
00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:36.903
I'm gonna put everything on this fucking on black.

630
00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:38.800
And hopefully I win.

631
00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:40.190
If not, I'm broke."

632
00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:42.090
I put my whole life...

633
00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:44.100
A guy that was scared of the fucking water,

634
00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:45.060
a guy that could fucking...

635
00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:46.620
Taught himself how to read and write.

636
00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.000
On being one of hardest motherfuckers on the planet.

637
00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.430
Think about that shit!

638
00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:52.330
A guy that came from nothing.

639
00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:53.747
I put my whole life.

640
00:25:53.747 --> 00:25:55.150
"Oh, I'm gonna go out here

641
00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.590
and put everything on David fucking Goggins

642
00:25:57.590 --> 00:25:59.640
to be a Navy SEAL.

643
00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:03.350
Not to go be a boy scout or some shit.

644
00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:05.030
A Navy SEAL."

645
00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:07.290
And I look at that.

646
00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:08.600
And I did all this shit

647
00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:12.483
just to get the opportunity to succeed.

648
00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:16.400
That's what people don't fucking understand, man.

649
00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:18.633
People see the end result.

650
00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:21.087
I remember that guy saying,

651
00:26:21.087 --> 00:26:23.220
"My God, man, I can't believe what the fuck I've just done.

652
00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:24.540
I put everything...

653
00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:25.800
Ruined relationships.

654
00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:26.930
Ruin this, ruin that,

655
00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:27.763
put everything on the fact

656
00:26:27.763 --> 00:26:30.080
that I have to become someone in this world,

657
00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:32.260
or I'm no good for anybody.

658
00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:34.110
And where does that come from?

659
00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:36.140
Where did that compulsion, that drive?

660
00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:38.390
It comes from a disgusting place

661
00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:42.120
of not being fulfilled in your life,

662
00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:47.120
of afraid of dying having never accomplished anything.

663
00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:49.830
That's a fear that some people run away from,

664
00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:51.980
that people don't wanna face.

665
00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:55.150
When you have a real fear of dying,

666
00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:57.580
and being just another person.

667
00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.120
That I live to pay the bills.

668
00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:01.360
I make $1,000 a month.

669
00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:02.230
This is my life.

670
00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:04.090
I pray for cockroaches, man.

671
00:27:04.090 --> 00:27:06.460
If that makes you feel good, that's great.

672
00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:08.230
It didn't make me feel good.

673
00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:11.641
I wanted to the first time in my life, after 26 years,

674
00:27:11.641 --> 00:27:13.610
it was 24, 25, wherever I was,

675
00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:16.100
I wanted to feel good about myself.

676
00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:20.420
And that was the ticket for it.

677
00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:24.933
Yeah, I mean, you have this huge reservoir,

678
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:31.040
this capacity to leverage pain and circumstance

679
00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:34.610
to drive change within yourself.

680
00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:36.920
To be able to not be a victim,

681
00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:38.850
but to look at pain as your friend,

682
00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.090
as a catalyst for growth.

683
00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:42.480
And I think there's a lot of people out there...

684
00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:44.490
Look, if you're in a enough pain,

685
00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.410
that's gonna move the needle for you.

686
00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:47.600
There's a lot of people out there

687
00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:50.100
that are in just enough pain

688
00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:52.320
where they're willing to just settle

689
00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:53.270
for what they have,

690
00:27:53.270 --> 00:27:55.610
because they're not in enough pain to change.

691
00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:58.590
And the fear of change outweighs the pain

692
00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:00.980
of their daily existence.

693
00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:03.620
You know, the one gift I have with all that being said,

694
00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:04.940
what you just said there,

695
00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:07.850
is I have the ability to see the end

696
00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.000
before the beginning even begins.

697
00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:10.833
And what that means

698
00:28:10.833 --> 00:28:12.880
is I know that to get to the very end,

699
00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:14.460
I can see it right now.

700
00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:15.723
So before I went to BUD/S,

701
00:28:15.723 --> 00:28:17.310
and I was losing all this fucking weight and shit,

702
00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:20.570
I saw myself walking across the fucking stage

703
00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:22.330
at 191 fucking pounds.

704
00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:25.310
That's what I had to get to, to get into the door.

705
00:28:25.310 --> 00:28:27.150
I saw myself...

706
00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:28.370
Six months, a year later,

707
00:28:28.370 --> 00:28:29.370
whatever it's gonna take me to do it.

708
00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:31.010
I saw myself walking across that stage,

709
00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.190
getting that certificate of graduation from BUD/S.

710
00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:38.533
And I was able to be there at 300 pounds.

711
00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:41.310
And that feeling...

712
00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:42.960
I was nowhere near that fucking feeling.

713
00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:45.180
I was able to put myself there

714
00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:47.197
a million times every fucking day.

715
00:28:47.197 --> 00:28:48.397
and that feeling of like,

716
00:28:48.397 --> 00:28:49.860
"Oh my God!"

717
00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:52.430
That is gonna feel fucking amazing.

718
00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.110
That's what made me suffer.

719
00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:56.800
That's what allowed the pain to be real.

720
00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:58.557
And I said, "This is worth it."

721
00:28:58.557 --> 00:29:01.726
"I wanna feel, for this fucking next 18 months..."

722
00:29:01.726 --> 00:29:04.067
'Cause it took me 18 fucking months

723
00:29:04.067 --> 00:29:06.133
to finally just get through BUD/S, 18 months.

724
00:29:06.133 --> 00:29:08.203
It's six months, took me 18.

725
00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:11.870
That's what woke me up every fucking morning,

726
00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:13.370
was I'm gonna put myself

727
00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:15.620
through this much fucking pain and suffering

728
00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:18.180
for a few seconds.

729
00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:19.830
That's all it is.

730
00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:22.030
A few seconds of joy,

731
00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:23.130
and it's so fucking worth it, man.

732
00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:24.750
That's what people don't get.

733
00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:27.410
So I'm able to put myself at the finish line,

734
00:29:27.410 --> 00:29:29.390
even though I have no finish line,

735
00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:31.670
but at the finish line of an event,

736
00:29:31.670 --> 00:29:33.210
before I even start the motherfucker,

737
00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:35.800
to say, "How are you gonna feel at the end of this?"

738
00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:38.530
Well, visualization is one of the challenges.

739
00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:42.800
And part of that is not just visualizing success,

740
00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:46.230
or living in the reality of achieving

741
00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.230
what you're setting out to achieve,

742
00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:50.890
but also visualizing how you're gonna navigate

743
00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:53.270
all the obstacles that are gonna get thrown in your path.

744
00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:56.660
Right, visualizing is my biggest tool of life.

745
00:29:56.660 --> 00:30:00.730
That's why I've been able to put myself in cold water,

746
00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:03.080
put myself in 100-mile race,

747
00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.097
millions of times before I've done it.

748
00:30:05.097 --> 00:30:07.080
And I've able to go through the race,

749
00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:08.980
and see how I'm gonna feel at mile 50,

750
00:30:09.902 --> 00:30:13.030
almost to the exact feeling.

751
00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:14.290
Right, so when it comes up,

752
00:30:14.290 --> 00:30:15.760
it's no surprise.
It's no surprise.

753
00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:17.620
I've already done this a million times.

754
00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:19.730
And that's the one thing I practice and practice

755
00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:21.340
and practice and practice overnight.

756
00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:23.370
But also, the most important thing

757
00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:26.330
is I practiced that feeling of accomplishment

758
00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:28.210
that I'm gonna have when it's all said and done with.

759
00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:30.330
The crazy thing about the ASVAB story,

760
00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:34.530
is that, in addition to having to pass this test

761
00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:37.810
and get that 50, you also had to lose 100 pounds

762
00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:41.110
in like 30 days or some crazy-short period of time?

763
00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:42.500
It was less than three months.

764
00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:44.570
Right, okay, three months. (clears throat)

765
00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:47.667
But you compartmentalize these two tasks and say,

766
00:30:47.667 --> 00:30:49.210
"Look, the first thing I gotta do

767
00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:50.630
is I gotta pass this test,

768
00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:53.610
because the weight doesn't matter if I don't get that 50."

769
00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:57.040
And you shelved or put off losing the weight

770
00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:58.980
to focus on the academic end of it,

771
00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:01.530
until you completed that hurdle,

772
00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:03.520
and then, you looked at the weight stuff?

773
00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:05.350
I'm like that now, just-
You still got it done?

774
00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:07.170
Yeah.
It's like being focused

775
00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:08.620
on one thing at a time.

776
00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.290
I have to be very present in everything I do.

777
00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:12.610
Like right now, I'm with Rich Roll.

778
00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:15.080
I'm not thinking about shit, but Rich Roll,

779
00:31:15.080 --> 00:31:17.333
and what the fucks coming outta your mouth right now.

780
00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.650
That's what gives me a huge advantage in life.

781
00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:22.530
Especially today, in this day and age,

782
00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.020
with so much shit going so fast,

783
00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:26.150
and everybody wants to keep everything going,

784
00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:27.460
everything up and everything.

785
00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:30.980
I wanna be the greatest multitasker of all time.

786
00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:32.620
Not me.

787
00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:36.400
If I put my 100% into what's in front of me,

788
00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:38.050
I will destroy it.

789
00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:39.930
If I'm out here, just multitasking and shit,

790
00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:41.660
I'm gonna half-assed in everything I do.

791
00:31:41.660 --> 00:31:43.920
So it is the most important thing in the world to me

792
00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:45.760
is being focused at the task at hand.

793
00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:47.590
And it's getting harder and harder to do that,

794
00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:49.520
because there are so many distractions

795
00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:52.570
and it's so easy to distract yourself.

796
00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:54.420
