is where's an area where you can handle the pain of the work

1314
00:51:39.890 --> 00:51:41.520
better than the people around you.

1315
00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:44.090
The area where you are more well-equipped to suffer,

1316
00:51:44.090 --> 00:51:45.800
is the work that you were made to do,

1317
00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:47.470
which is an interesting way to think about it, right?

1318
00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:48.307
Like most people think about,

1319
00:51:48.307 --> 00:51:49.670
"Oh, well, where is it just easy?

1320
00:51:49.670 --> 00:51:50.930
Where do I succeed?"

1321
00:51:50.930 --> 00:51:53.900
But every area requires hard work and effort

1322
00:51:53.900 --> 00:51:55.860
to achieve some level of success.

1323
00:51:55.860 --> 00:51:57.760
So the question is not, "Where is it easy?"

1324
00:51:57.760 --> 00:51:59.530
The question is, "Where can I handle the pain?"

1325
00:51:59.530 --> 00:52:00.780
Mm-hmm.

1326
00:52:00.780 --> 00:52:03.970
Yeah, it's a different lens through which to look at it,

1327
00:52:03.970 --> 00:52:07.853
but I think that's right, you know?

1328
00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:11.810
For some people for whatever reason,

1329
00:52:11.810 --> 00:52:14.020
people who grow up and become great writers,

1330
00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:14.853
they...
(James scoffing)

1331
00:52:14.853 --> 00:52:15.820
Writing is suffering,

1332
00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:18.440
but they can handle it for some reason.

1333
00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:21.320
Navy SEALS, like it's not easy to be one,

1334
00:52:21.320 --> 00:52:22.670
but the guys who can make it,

1335
00:52:22.670 --> 00:52:24.880
somehow they can handle the suffering of it.

1336
00:52:24.880 --> 00:52:29.490
Yeah, they're well-suited and prepared and willing

1337
00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:31.800
to undergo that for some reason.

1338
00:52:31.800 --> 00:52:33.120
And I think I used that line,

1339
00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:34.840
or something similar to that at the end of that chapter,

1340
00:52:34.840 --> 00:52:37.220
which is at the peak of any field,

1341
00:52:37.220 --> 00:52:38.270
what you're going to find

1342
00:52:38.270 --> 00:52:41.230
are people who are both well-suited and well-trained.

1343
00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:42.950
It's not just one or the other.

1344
00:52:42.950 --> 00:52:44.810
They have the environment matching,

1345
00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:46.720
and they have the hard work and the effort

1346
00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:48.767
and the perseverance.
Right.

1347
00:52:48.767 --> 00:52:52.400
"Habits are the compound interest of self-improvement."

1348
00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:53.233
I love it.

1349
00:52:53.233 --> 00:52:54.072
That's one of my favorite lines in the book.

1350
00:52:54.072 --> 00:52:54.905
It's a great line.

1351
00:52:54.905 --> 00:52:57.010
I think it encapsulates the core idea, right?

1352
00:52:57.010 --> 00:53:00.320
That if you're willing to build those small behaviors

1353
00:53:00.320 --> 00:53:03.160
and layer 1% improvements on top of each other,

1354
00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:05.030
they will compound and multiply,

1355
00:53:05.030 --> 00:53:06.400
the same way that money multiplies

1356
00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:07.450
through compound interest,

1357
00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:09.010
the effects of your habits multiply,

1358
00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:10.460
as you repeat them over time.

1359
00:53:10.460 --> 00:53:13.800
And that can be true for you or against you.

1360
00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:16.060
And that's why it's crucial to understand how habits work,

1361
00:53:16.060 --> 00:53:18.090
so that you can make sure

1362
00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:19.990
that they're multiplying in your favor,

1363
00:53:19.990 --> 00:53:21.990
rather than to your detriment.
(air whooshing)

1364
00:53:21.990 --> 00:53:24.230
It is time for me to introduce you

1365
00:53:24.230 --> 00:53:26.490
to the priestess of self-acceptance,

1366
00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:31.390
of body positivity and fun, ultrarunner Mirna Valerio.

1367
00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:34.210
Easily one of the most inspirational athletes I've ever met,

1368
00:53:34.210 --> 00:53:36.880
Mirna is a true ambassador of sport,

1369
00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:38.410
on a mission to empower humans

1370
00:53:38.410 --> 00:53:40.880
of all shapes, sizes, colors and genders,

1371
00:53:40.880 --> 00:53:43.570
to proudly embrace their bodies,

1372
00:53:43.570 --> 00:53:47.950
expand their horizon and own their personal truth.

1373
00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:48.783
In this clip,

1374
00:53:48.783 --> 00:53:50.230
she demands that we broaden our minds,

1375
00:53:50.230 --> 00:53:52.230
and crush preconceived ideas

1376
00:53:52.230 --> 00:53:53.896
of what our bodies are capable of.

1377
00:53:53.896 --> 00:53:55.830
(air whooshing)

1378
00:53:55.830 --> 00:53:58.350
When that cardiologist told me that I was gonna die

1379
00:53:58.350 --> 00:54:00.210
if I didn't change my lifestyle,

1380
00:54:00.210 --> 00:54:03.970
I really did make a decision to change things.

1381
00:54:03.970 --> 00:54:06.160
I prioritized my day.

1382
00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:07.840
I stopped bringing work home,

1383
00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:10.020
which is really difficult to do

1384
00:54:10.020 --> 00:54:11.770
when you work in a boarding school.

1385
00:54:13.937 --> 00:54:15.380
I would get up super early,

1386
00:54:15.380 --> 00:54:19.390
and hop on the treadmill for an hour and then do Pilates,

1387
00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:24.190
and then do awful (laughs) Biggest Loser videos.

1388
00:54:24.190 --> 00:54:26.240
Uh-huh.
Really, really awful.

1389
00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:27.563
But I did them anyway,

1390
00:54:28.890 --> 00:54:33.100
because I needed to change drastically.

1391
00:54:33.100 --> 00:54:34.960
And so, I mean, and I did.

1392
00:54:34.960 --> 00:54:37.080
I would work out for five hours a day.

1393
00:54:37.080 --> 00:54:37.913
Wow!
Yeah,

1394
00:54:37.913 --> 00:54:41.440
I was really committed to changing my life

1395
00:54:42.470 --> 00:54:45.420
in a very drastic way, so that's what I did.

1396
00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:46.253
Right.

1397
00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:48.856
Little did you know.
Who knew-

1398
00:54:48.856 --> 00:54:49.922
This was set in motion,
(Mirna laughing)

1399
00:54:49.922 --> 00:54:53.050
this entire new life that you live now, right?

1400
00:54:53.050 --> 00:54:54.224
Who knew?

1401
00:54:54.224 --> 00:54:56.190
What was the big...

1402
00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:57.983
Was there a breakthrough moment,

1403
00:54:58.986 --> 00:55:00.840
where you really just embraced this idea

1404
00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:02.570
that I am a runner,

1405
00:55:02.570 --> 00:55:06.203
and this is the path that I'm gonna blaze for myself?

1406
00:55:07.580 --> 00:55:09.770
I don't think there was one moment.

1407
00:55:09.770 --> 00:55:14.770
I think I just slowly grew into a running persona.

1408
00:55:15.400 --> 00:55:17.350
And I never really had any qualms

1409
00:55:17.350 --> 00:55:19.283
about whether or not I was a runner.

1410
00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:23.980
I ran, therefore I was a runner,

1411
00:55:23.980 --> 00:55:28.980
but as far as the really deep running persona,

1412
00:55:30.841 --> 00:55:33.620
and identifying deeply as a runner,

1413
00:55:33.620 --> 00:55:36.513
I think that just came about organically.

1414
00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:41.120
And then, it's always a surprise to me

1415
00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:43.540
that people who run don't think they're runners.

1416
00:55:43.540 --> 00:55:48.540
Because if you run, you use your body for running,

1417
00:55:48.740 --> 00:55:50.260
(laughs) it's something you do regularly-

1418
00:55:50.260 --> 00:55:53.100
Then you are what you do?
Then you're a runner.

1419
00:55:53.100 --> 00:55:54.260
Right?
Right.

1420
00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:57.790
We all measure ourselves up against some idealized version

1421
00:55:57.790 --> 00:56:00.530
of what that means or is,

1422
00:56:00.530 --> 00:56:02.240
and the truth is,

1423
00:56:02.240 --> 00:56:06.210
99.99% of people that are out running (laughs)

1424
00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:09.123
are not winning marathons and things like that.

1425
00:56:09.123 --> 00:56:11.650
This is the greatest participation sport.

1426
00:56:11.650 --> 00:56:14.280
You know, as I said, I'm just a person who likes to run,

1427
00:56:14.280 --> 00:56:15.950
and I like to exercise.

1428
00:56:15.950 --> 00:56:18.590
But on the other hand,

1429
00:56:18.590 --> 00:56:21.000
I know that there are lots of people

1430
00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:23.270
for whom like this thing that I'm doing

1431
00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:25.890
seems to be inaccessible to them,

1432
00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:29.420
because they have this idea that a runner looks this way.

1433
00:56:29.420 --> 00:56:30.943
A runner runs this quickly.

1434
00:56:32.248 --> 00:56:36.120
A person who hikes is a certain body type,

1435
00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:38.713
a certain race, probably male.

1436
00:56:40.941 --> 00:56:42.740
And so when they see me,

1437
00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:46.380
it blows their minds that I'm out there

1438
00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:47.883
doing those same things,

1439
00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:53.030
unapologetically and without regard

1440
00:56:53.030 --> 00:56:57.710
for what people think I should be doing

1441
00:56:57.710 --> 00:56:59.860
and where people think I should be doing it.

1442
00:56:59.860 --> 00:57:02.190
The unapologetic part is a big part of it too.

1443
00:57:02.190 --> 00:57:04.030
Like you always have this huge smile,

1444
00:57:04.030 --> 00:57:06.560
and you're the life of the party on the trail.

1445
00:57:06.560 --> 00:57:07.393
And I think-

1446
00:57:07.393 --> 00:57:08.460
I don't know about the life of the party. (laughs)

1447
00:57:08.460 --> 00:57:09.557
Well, there's this sense like,

1448
00:57:09.557 --> 00:57:12.260
"Oh, well, I'm here, but I really shouldn't be here."

1449
00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:15.207
Like the sheepishness that perhaps somebody else

1450
00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:19.050
who's trying to make this work

1451
00:57:19.050 --> 00:57:21.295
would feel in that experience.

1452
00:57:21.295 --> 00:57:22.220
Well, I definitely...

1453
00:57:22.220 --> 00:57:24.750
When I started trail running,

1454
00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:27.315
I didn't know what I was doing. (laughs)

1455
00:57:27.315 --> 00:57:28.523
Nobody does.

1456
00:57:28.523 --> 00:57:29.356
Right.

1457
00:57:29.356 --> 00:57:31.180
I still don't know what I'm doing.

1458
00:57:31.180 --> 00:57:34.780
But I would just kind of like hang in the back

1459
00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:37.870
and listen to the race directors and then,

1460
00:57:37.870 --> 00:57:39.180
go off on an adventure,

1461
00:57:39.180 --> 00:57:41.120
because I always see it as an adventure.

1462
00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:43.780
And again, not knowing what I was doing,

1463
00:57:43.780 --> 00:57:45.913
not knowing what I was in for.

1464
00:57:48.300 --> 00:57:53.190
But as I became used to that and more comfortable with that,

1465
00:57:57.700 --> 00:58:01.130
the unknown aspect of trail running,

1466
00:58:01.130 --> 00:58:03.523
and anything that you do in the outdoors,

1467
00:58:04.420 --> 00:58:06.980
I definitely became more comfortable

1468
00:58:06.980 --> 00:58:09.460
just being in those spaces.

1469
00:58:09.460 --> 00:58:14.460
And that's how I operate in any uncomfortable situation.

1470
00:58:15.110 --> 00:58:18.860
I'll hang in the back and observe, learn things.

1471
00:58:18.860 --> 00:58:22.080
And then, as I become more comfortable, I...

1472
00:58:24.660 --> 00:58:26.930
What's the word I'm looking for? (laughs)

1473
00:58:26.930 --> 00:58:28.550
Acclimate?
Well, I acclimate,

1474
00:58:28.550 --> 00:58:30.780
but I extend myself more and more

1475
00:58:32.080 --> 00:58:33.320
when I'm in those situations.

1476
00:58:33.320 --> 00:58:35.430
Yeah, and are you still doing the...

1477
00:58:35.430 --> 00:58:37.120
You've got these running retreats,

1478
00:58:37.120 --> 00:58:39.490
the Slow As Fuck running retreats

1479
00:58:39.490 --> 00:58:41.364
Did you actually do these, or-
I did.

1480
00:58:41.364 --> 00:58:43.520
You did? (laughs)
I absolutely did.

1481
00:58:43.520 --> 00:58:44.930
That's like the best name ever.

1482
00:58:44.930 --> 00:58:47.630
They sold out over and over again-

1483
00:58:47.630 --> 00:58:50.630
'Cause it's like, all the fear that people have,

1484
00:58:50.630 --> 00:58:51.606
like, "I can't do running retreat."

1485
00:58:51.606 --> 00:58:53.156
It immediately tears down a wall.

1486
00:58:53.156 --> 00:58:54.061
Right.

1487
00:58:54.061 --> 00:58:55.870
"Oh, well I think I can do that."

1488
00:58:55.870 --> 00:58:58.010
"I'm slow as fuck." (laughs)
Yeah, exactly.

1489
00:58:58.010 --> 00:58:58.843
Exactly.

1490
00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:00.753
What are those about?

1491
00:59:00.753 --> 00:59:03.960
Where do you do those
Okay, so... (laughs)

1492
00:59:03.960 --> 00:59:04.793
So yes,

1493
00:59:04.793 --> 00:59:06.400
they are called Slow As Fuck Trail Running Adventures.

1494
00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:08.470
(Mirna laughing)

1495
00:59:08.470 --> 00:59:13.470
And I created them specifically to serve a community

1496
00:59:17.800 --> 00:59:19.610
that is made up of runners.

1497
00:59:19.610 --> 00:59:22.590
Some of them are plus-size runners, some of them are not,

1498
00:59:22.590 --> 00:59:25.000
but everybody's slow. (laughs)

1499
00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:28.950
And by slow, I mean, we don't run 10 minute miles-

1500
00:59:28.950 --> 00:59:31.070
What if you're fast and you wanna go?

1501
00:59:31.070 --> 00:59:33.017
I tell people, I said, "This is not for you."

1502
00:59:33.017 --> 00:59:35.427
"This is not the experience for you."

1503
00:59:35.427 --> 00:59:37.740
"Because we're going to be on the trails.

1504
00:59:37.740 --> 00:59:42.460
We will run, we will walk, we will take selfies.

1505
00:59:42.460 --> 00:59:43.970
We will have a picnic.

1506
00:59:43.970 --> 00:59:45.350
It'll be a whole-day thing.

1507
00:59:45.350 --> 00:59:46.550
This is not competitive.

1508
00:59:47.730 --> 00:59:49.650
You should not consider this as training,

1509
00:59:49.650 --> 00:59:52.300
because we're just gonna...

1510
00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:54.140
We're gonna play it by ear.

1511
00:59:54.140 --> 00:59:55.020
Yeah, play it by ear.

1512
00:59:55.020 --> 00:59:58.310
And yes, pick daisies, smell them.

1513
00:59:58.310 --> 01:00:00.023
We're going to climb mountains.

1514
01:00:01.156 --> 01:00:04.113
We're gonna do some sort of reflection work,

1515
01:00:05.380 --> 01:00:06.730
and we're have a good time.

1516
01:00:08.232 --> 01:00:09.065
So yeah-

1517
01:00:09.065 --> 01:00:09.898
How many people?

1518
01:00:09.898 --> 01:00:12.150
The very first one I did was 22 people,

1519
01:00:12.150 --> 01:00:14.576
which was a lot of people. (laughs)

1520
01:00:14.576 --> 01:00:16.993
And do you just do it in your backyard trails,

1521
01:00:16.993 --> 01:00:18.673
or you go somewhere?

1522
01:00:18.673 --> 01:00:23.673
I rent a house somewhere and that's near a lot of trails,

1523
01:00:25.421 --> 01:00:29.370
and I contract a lot of people in to do yoga,

1524
01:00:29.370 --> 01:00:31.561
meditation, to...

1525
01:00:31.561 --> 01:00:33.530
I had Roz Mays, who's a pole dancer,

1526
01:00:33.530 --> 01:00:37.373
come in and do some sensual movement without a pole,

1527
01:00:38.450 --> 01:00:42.150
and with the goal being that people get more comfortable

1528
01:00:43.820 --> 01:00:46.340
in their own bodies and in the space that they inhabit,

1529
01:00:46.340 --> 01:00:48.390
and they transfer that out onto the trails,

1530
01:00:48.390 --> 01:00:49.870
and hopefully, in the rest of their lives.

1531
01:00:49.870 --> 01:00:51.934
And so that was fun.

1532
01:00:51.934 --> 01:00:55.240
(Mirna laughing)
(air whooshing)

1533
01:00:55.240 --> 01:00:57.310
No mindset deep dive would be complete

1534
01:00:57.310 --> 01:00:59.310
without a word from my next guest,

1535
01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:02.450
the irrepressible James Lawrence.

1536
01:01:02.450 --> 01:01:04.010
AKA The Iron Cowboy,

1537
01:01:04.010 --> 01:01:07.210
James is a multiple world record-crushing endurance athlete,

1538
01:01:07.210 --> 01:01:09.180
who's mind boggling feats

1539
01:01:09.180 --> 01:01:14.180
include completing 50 Ironman distance triathlons,

1540
01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:16.900
in 50 days, in 50 states,

1541
01:01:16.900 --> 01:01:17.880
and most recently,

1542
01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:20.720
completing in absolutely astonishing

1543
01:01:20.720 --> 01:01:25.720
101 Iron Distance Triathlons in 101 consecutive days.

1544
01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:29.380
In this clip, he explains

1545
01:01:29.380 --> 01:01:32.554
how he fosters his extraordinary mental conviction.

1546
01:01:32.554 --> 01:01:34.580
(air whooshing)

1547
01:01:34.580 --> 01:01:36.860
Your gift, really, is that you're able to suffer,

1548
01:01:36.860 --> 01:01:41.860
but you have figured out a way to express that

1549
01:01:42.020 --> 01:01:43.360
through these challenges

1550
01:01:43.360 --> 01:01:45.530
that get incrementally more and more difficult

1551
01:01:45.530 --> 01:01:46.363
over the years,

1552
01:01:46.363 --> 01:01:49.360
that allow you to be in the place that you're in now.

1553
01:01:49.360 --> 01:01:51.570
I didn't say that very articulately,

1554
01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:52.990
but you know what I'm saying?

1555
01:01:52.990 --> 01:01:56.150
This starts on a Ferris wheel for 10 days.

1556
01:01:56.150 --> 01:01:59.070
And it's not like you're out winning triathlons

1557
01:01:59.070 --> 01:02:00.550
right away or anything like that.

1558
01:02:00.550 --> 01:02:03.940
Like you have a very different path into this kind of thing.

1559
01:02:03.940 --> 01:02:08.780
I think that is the most beautiful or profound lesson

1560
01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:10.780
that needs to be heard today.

1561
01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:14.780
Nobody's the expert at the beginning of their journey.

1562
01:02:14.780 --> 01:02:15.980
Nobody is.

1563
01:02:15.980 --> 01:02:19.080
And you have to meet yourself where you're at today,

1564
01:02:19.080 --> 01:02:20.973
and that be the expectation.

1565
01:02:22.450 --> 01:02:25.300
The hardest thing to do is to start on any journey,

1566
01:02:25.300 --> 01:02:28.020
because that's where the highest amount of struggle is.

1567
01:02:28.020 --> 01:02:31.050
That's where our experience and our momentum and our success

1568
01:02:31.050 --> 01:02:33.400
is at its lowest.

1569
01:02:33.400 --> 01:02:35.380
And a lot of people see the headline,

1570
01:02:35.380 --> 01:02:36.237
the 100, and think,

1571
01:02:36.237 --> 01:02:40.440
"Oh, he's too just born to do this kinda thing."

1572
01:02:40.440 --> 01:02:42.770
And I love saying it now

1573
01:02:42.770 --> 01:02:45.570
that you can't go from zero to 100

1574
01:02:45.570 --> 01:02:50.570
and miss all of the moments in between.

1575
01:02:50.960 --> 01:02:53.530
And everybody has a beginning, middle and end

1576
01:02:53.530 --> 01:02:54.363
to their journey.

1577
01:02:54.363 --> 01:02:55.830
A lot of people just get caught up

1578
01:02:55.830 --> 01:02:58.940
in needing to understand how it's gonna unfold,

1579
01:02:58.940 --> 01:03:02.180
and they get paralyzed and never actually move forward.

1580
01:03:02.180 --> 01:03:06.880
And too, that was beautifully said,

1581
01:03:06.880 --> 01:03:09.720
but they're too...

1582
01:03:09.720 --> 01:03:12.270
And social media, this is the bad part about social media

1583
01:03:12.270 --> 01:03:13.610
is they're comparing themselves

1584
01:03:13.610 --> 01:03:15.640
against what other people are doing

1585
01:03:15.640 --> 01:03:17.320
or showing that they're doing,

1586
01:03:17.320 --> 01:03:18.860
and they say, "I can't do that.

1587
01:03:18.860 --> 01:03:19.693
I'm a good enough."

1588
01:03:19.693 --> 01:03:22.090
And that's what stops them from starting.

1589
01:03:22.090 --> 01:03:24.010
And that's tragic to me,

1590
01:03:24.010 --> 01:03:26.920
because thankfully social media wasn't big

1591
01:03:27.760 --> 01:03:30.060
when we started and I didn't have a basis of comparison

1592
01:03:30.060 --> 01:03:32.430
and people were like, "Oh, who are your mentors?"

1593
01:03:32.430 --> 01:03:33.900
And who's this and that, and who do you look up to?

1594
01:03:33.900 --> 01:03:36.220
And I was like, I would not compare myself

1595
01:03:36.220 --> 01:03:38.057
with what the current standard of excellence was,

1596
01:03:38.057 --> 01:03:39.230
and I would go out,

1597
01:03:39.230 --> 01:03:41.773
and try to do what I believe was possible.

1598
01:03:42.740 --> 01:03:44.590
And here's the perfect example.

1599
01:03:44.590 --> 01:03:48.870
At the time when I said I was gonna do the 50,

1600
01:03:48.870 --> 01:03:53.280
the baseline was kind of like the EPIC5, right?

1601
01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:55.757
And had I looked at that and said,

1602
01:03:55.757 --> 01:03:58.950
"Oh, the EPIC5 is the standard of excellence."

1603
01:03:58.950 --> 01:04:01.027
At the time, which it was, I would've said,

1604
01:04:01.027 --> 01:04:04.830
"Okay, I'm gonna double that standard to do 10."

1605
01:04:04.830 --> 01:04:06.890
So why did I go all the way to 50?

1606
01:04:06.890 --> 01:04:08.260
Because for me personally,

1607
01:04:08.260 --> 01:04:09.980
I wasn't comparing myself

1608
01:04:09.980 --> 01:04:13.170
about what others were doing and accomplishing.

1609
01:04:13.170 --> 01:04:15.830
I went out and I set the standard or the bar

1610
01:04:15.830 --> 01:04:18.250
to what I thought I could do.

1611
01:04:18.250 --> 01:04:20.260
And that's where people get into problems and struggles

1612
01:04:20.260 --> 01:04:23.880
is they are now comparing themselves

1613
01:04:23.880 --> 01:04:27.630
to what they see the top guys doing,

1614
01:04:27.630 --> 01:04:30.450
and they just get overwhelmed and they don't start.

1615
01:04:30.450 --> 01:04:32.700
And so there's two sides to that coin, right?

1616
01:04:33.810 --> 01:04:36.210
Don't get sidelined by what you see people are doing,

1617
01:04:36.210 --> 01:04:37.860
and then, don't look what other people doing

1618
01:04:37.860 --> 01:04:40.720
and do what you believe is possible in yourself.

1619
01:04:40.720 --> 01:04:42.710
And I couldn't have done what I did mentally,

1620
01:04:42.710 --> 01:04:45.520
without learning and struggling

1621
01:04:45.520 --> 01:04:47.570
and developing and sharpening that thing.

1622
01:04:48.560 --> 01:04:52.130
The big question topic, are you born with it?

1623
01:04:52.130 --> 01:04:53.507
Or can you develop it?

1624
01:04:53.507 --> 01:04:57.363
And my answer, just based on my personal experiences,

1625
01:04:58.350 --> 01:04:59.803
it's nurture.

1626
01:05:01.800 --> 01:05:02.920
You have to develop it.

1627
01:05:02.920 --> 01:05:04.330
You have to work on it.

1628
01:05:04.330 --> 01:05:07.083
Now, we're all born with a different baseline.

1629
01:05:08.480 --> 01:05:10.080
Jace would be a great example.

1630
01:05:10.080 --> 01:05:12.910
He obviously has a different starting baseline

1631
01:05:12.910 --> 01:05:16.060
for mental toughness than some other people do.

1632
01:05:16.060 --> 01:05:17.300
Now, if he-
And you're the kid

1633
01:05:17.300 --> 01:05:20.400
who was on the Ferris wheel for 10 days to win a prize.

1634
01:05:20.400 --> 01:05:21.233
Right, exactly.

1635
01:05:21.233 --> 01:05:23.246
So your baseline is probably a little bit different.

1636
01:05:23.246 --> 01:05:24.692
Like, how old were you for that?

1637
01:05:24.692 --> 01:05:26.400
20, 22.
22, 20-

1638
01:05:26.400 --> 01:05:30.336
21,22, but I took that knowledge from that experience,

1639
01:05:30.336 --> 01:05:32.820
and then I've really showed up in my life and sharpened it.

1640
01:05:32.820 --> 01:05:34.380
Jace has an incredible opportunity

1641
01:05:34.380 --> 01:05:38.130
to be something very special in that mindset space.

1642
01:05:38.130 --> 01:05:39.340
And I come across...

1643
01:05:39.340 --> 01:05:41.540
You can pick out, okay, baseline higher,

1644
01:05:41.540 --> 01:05:42.770
baselines higher, baselines higher,

1645
01:05:42.770 --> 01:05:44.240
and develop that talent.

1646
01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:46.220
But everybody can develop it.

1647
01:05:46.220 --> 01:05:50.370
'Cause I'm a great example of this is a mental journey.

1648
01:05:50.370 --> 01:05:51.970
I'm not the most physically gifted.

1649
01:05:51.970 --> 01:05:55.110
Now, the other big question is like,

1650
01:05:55.110 --> 01:05:58.850
how much is physical and how much is mental for what we do.

1651
01:05:58.850 --> 01:06:00.770
or the journey that we're on?

1652
01:06:00.770 --> 01:06:03.303
And my answer has changed over the years.

1653
01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:06.570
And you hear a lot of people have different opinions.

1654
01:06:06.570 --> 01:06:09.820
It's like 70% mental and...

1655
01:06:09.820 --> 01:06:14.820
It's 100% physical and it's 100% mental.

1656
01:06:15.900 --> 01:06:20.620
You cannot do what I did, if I'm very strong mentally,

1657
01:06:20.620 --> 01:06:23.540
and I'm a 300-pound man.

1658
01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:27.760
Mentally, you cannot drag your body through that experience.

1659
01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:32.760
And if you're extremely physical, but have a weak mind,

1660
01:06:35.150 --> 01:06:36.480
I've seen so many athletes

1661
01:06:36.480 --> 01:06:38.570
that are way more talented than I am fail

1662
01:06:38.570 --> 01:06:40.830
because they don't have the mental component of it.

1663
01:06:40.830 --> 01:06:45.520
And so you straight up have to be 100% on both of them

1664
01:06:45.520 --> 01:06:48.490
in order to accomplish something like that, or the journey

1665
01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:50.960
or push your boundaries or your limits, right?

1666
01:06:50.960 --> 01:06:52.480
It's a combination.

1667
01:06:52.480 --> 01:06:54.900
You cannot rely solely on one or the other,

1668
01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:57.970
and allow that to be, to get you through.

1669
01:06:57.970 --> 01:07:01.380
You have so strong at both of them or it just doesn't work.

1670
01:07:01.380 --> 01:07:02.390
Yeah.

1671
01:07:02.390 --> 01:07:05.900
It's really a habit or a muscle,

1672
01:07:05.900 --> 01:07:10.900
this idea of reflexively putting yourself into positions

1673
01:07:10.990 --> 01:07:13.640
where you're going to be tested

1674
01:07:13.640 --> 01:07:16.980
mentally, emotionally, physically, in all of these ways.

1675
01:07:16.980 --> 01:07:18.220
And we're in a culture right now,

1676
01:07:18.220 --> 01:07:22.150
where it's kinda easy to opt out of that type of situation.

1677
01:07:22.150 --> 01:07:26.060
So you have to almost have a little bit of extra gumption

1678
01:07:26.060 --> 01:07:29.220
to seek it out and put yourself in that situation.

1679
01:07:29.220 --> 01:07:32.220
Sunny always says, and she is not lying when she says it.

1680
01:07:32.220 --> 01:07:34.250
She says, if I have two paths in front of me,

1681
01:07:34.250 --> 01:07:36.370
I'm going to intentionally pick the harder one,

1682
01:07:36.370 --> 01:07:38.470
because I want it to hurt.

1683
01:07:38.470 --> 01:07:39.460
I wanna struggle.

1684
01:07:39.460 --> 01:07:40.293
I wanna learn.

1685
01:07:40.293 --> 01:07:41.560
I wanna grow.

1686
01:07:41.560 --> 01:07:42.393
And that's the exact opposite

1687
01:07:42.393 --> 01:07:44.240
of what most people are doing today.

1688
01:07:44.240 --> 01:07:46.690
They see two things and go, "That's easy route.

1689
01:07:46.690 --> 01:07:47.523
I'm gonna take that way."

1690
01:07:47.523 --> 01:07:49.160
And you're diluted into thinking

1691
01:07:49.160 --> 01:07:51.360
that the happy life is the easy life,

1692
01:07:51.360 --> 01:07:53.140
but the happy life, the fulfilled life,

1693
01:07:53.140 --> 01:07:54.330
the purpose-driven life,

1694
01:07:54.330 --> 01:07:58.170
is the life that invites those difficult situations

1695
01:07:58.170 --> 01:07:59.003
into your experience.

1696
01:07:59.003 --> 01:08:00.597
Well, when you're depressed and have anxiety, I'm like,

1697
01:08:00.597 --> 01:08:01.930
"I'm sure you do."

1698
01:08:01.930 --> 01:08:04.780
Because you're just taking that easy mountain bike path

1699
01:08:04.780 --> 01:08:07.670
that's just soft dirt, and all you do is...

1700
01:08:07.670 --> 01:08:08.503
There's no challenge.

1701
01:08:08.503 --> 01:08:11.660
There's no reason to develop any character,

1702
01:08:11.660 --> 01:08:13.200
but they're choosing that, right?

1703
01:08:13.200 --> 01:08:15.360
So it's like, "Well, I'm sure you feel depressed.

1704
01:08:15.360 --> 01:08:17.980
Like you're accomplishing nothing hard."

1705
01:08:17.980 --> 01:08:20.527
So that's definitely what our culture

1706
01:08:20.527 --> 01:08:23.180
and our society encourages.

1707
01:08:23.180 --> 01:08:24.013
Yeah.
Is the easy path.

1708
01:08:24.013 --> 01:08:25.240
One of the biggest things you learn

1709
01:08:25.240 --> 01:08:29.553
by taking the harder path is problem solving.

1710
01:08:30.910 --> 01:08:33.770
And that's what our team and Sunny's become experts at,

1711
01:08:33.770 --> 01:08:36.240
is problem-solving.

1712
01:08:36.240 --> 01:08:37.073
That's all we do.

1713
01:08:37.073 --> 01:08:38.910
We've become master problem solvers.

1714
01:08:38.910 --> 01:08:41.230
And we've learned how to problem-solve

1715
01:08:41.230 --> 01:08:42.200
in the face of adversity.

1716
01:08:42.200 --> 01:08:44.550
And when you take that path of least resistance,

1717
01:08:44.550 --> 01:08:47.890
there's very limited opportunities, or necessity,

1718
01:08:47.890 --> 01:08:49.090
to problem-solve.

1719
01:08:49.090 --> 01:08:50.730
And so you don't learn that skillset.

1720
01:08:50.730 --> 01:08:53.360
And life is about managing adversity,

1721
01:08:53.360 --> 01:08:55.858
and learning how to problem-solve at a high level.

1722
01:08:55.858 --> 01:08:57.670
(air whooshing)

1723
01:08:57.670 --> 01:08:59.530
The way we navigate our inner world,

1724
01:08:59.530 --> 01:09:01.920
our everyday thoughts, our emotions

1725
01:09:01.920 --> 01:09:05.260
and the stories we tell ourselves about ourselves,

1726
01:09:05.260 --> 01:09:07.750
is the single most important determinant

1727
01:09:07.750 --> 01:09:09.420
of our life's success.

1728
01:09:09.420 --> 01:09:12.300
In other words, emotional agility,

1729
01:09:12.300 --> 01:09:15.860
or the ability to confront difficult emotions,

1730
01:09:15.860 --> 01:09:18.820
gain critical self-insight from our feelings,

1731
01:09:18.820 --> 01:09:19.940
and ultimately,

1732
01:09:19.940 --> 01:09:24.340
use this newfound awareness to adaptively align our actions

1733
01:09:24.340 --> 01:09:25.720
with our values,

1734
01:09:25.720 --> 01:09:27.410
is how we make changes

1735
01:09:27.410 --> 01:09:31.150
to bring out the best of ourselves, moving forward.

1736
01:09:31.150 --> 01:09:32.910
This is the life's work

1737
01:09:32.910 --> 01:09:35.380
of award-winning psychologist on faculty

1738
01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:39.350
at Harvard Medical School, Susan David, Ph.D.

1739
01:09:39.350 --> 01:09:42.250
Because the tough emotions are the price we pay

1740
01:09:42.250 --> 01:09:44.100
for a meaningful life,

1741
01:09:44.100 --> 01:09:45.880
here's some evidence-based wisdom

1742
01:09:45.880 --> 01:09:50.167
on what it takes to hone that emotional agility.

1743
01:09:50.167 --> 01:09:52.300
(air whooshing)

1744
01:09:52.300 --> 01:09:57.300
So my work really traverses emotions.

1745
01:09:58.040 --> 01:10:00.630
So both the physical sensations

1746
01:10:00.630 --> 01:10:03.090
and the physiological sensations, and also the feelings.

1747
01:10:03.090 --> 01:10:05.527
So when we then construct something that says,

1748
01:10:05.527 --> 01:10:08.270
"I am sad because of something",

1749
01:10:08.270 --> 01:10:10.230
the feeling, the thought that we might have,

1750
01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:11.780
self-doubts, and the stories.

1751
01:10:11.780 --> 01:10:14.010
So my work traverses this whole idea

1752
01:10:14.010 --> 01:10:17.410
that our inner world, our thoughts,

1753
01:10:17.410 --> 01:10:19.860
our emotions and our stories,

1754
01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:24.370
often drive every aspect of how we love, how we live,

1755
01:10:24.370 --> 01:10:25.750
how we parent and how we lead.

1756
01:10:25.750 --> 01:10:27.930
And yet,

1757
01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:32.220
so much of the writing that exists on success,

1758
01:10:32.220 --> 01:10:33.490
is effectively writing that's

1759
01:10:33.490 --> 01:10:36.150
either about set goals and achieve them,

1760
01:10:36.150 --> 01:10:39.860
or about the landscape of what success looks like,

1761
01:10:39.860 --> 01:10:42.190
but there's very little that focuses.

1762
01:10:42.190 --> 01:10:43.770
I think, in an evidence-based way,

1763
01:10:43.770 --> 01:10:44.980
not to say that there isn't any,

1764
01:10:44.980 --> 01:10:47.580
but in an evidence-based and research-based way,

1765
01:10:47.580 --> 01:10:49.470
about what it takes internally,

1766
01:10:49.470 --> 01:10:50.570
in the way we deal

1767
01:10:50.570 --> 01:10:52.500
with our thoughts, our emotions and our stories.

1768
01:10:52.500 --> 01:10:53.719
Right.

1769
01:10:53.719 --> 01:10:57.930
And I think that begins with really understanding

1770
01:10:57.930 --> 01:11:00.480
that as these emotions well up inside of us,

1771
01:11:00.480 --> 01:11:01.313
as they do,

1772
01:11:01.313 --> 01:11:03.870
that they are part and parcel of what it means to be human.

1773
01:11:03.870 --> 01:11:05.760
They're entirely natural.

1774
01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:09.630
And the starting point is really to discern the fact

1775
01:11:09.630 --> 01:11:12.430
that you have a choice when they arise

1776
01:11:12.430 --> 01:11:13.380
as to how you behave.

1777
01:11:13.380 --> 01:11:15.040
That you don't need to necessarily

1778
01:11:15.040 --> 01:11:16.550
self-identify with them,

1779
01:11:16.550 --> 01:11:19.600
to the extent that they become that predictor of behavior,

1780
01:11:19.600 --> 01:11:20.870
that leads you astray.

1781
01:11:20.870 --> 01:11:21.770
Is that fair to say?
Absolutely.

1782
01:11:21.770 --> 01:11:24.560
Yeah, so the first point that you make,

1783
01:11:24.560 --> 01:11:27.670
which is that they're naturally occurring experiences

1784
01:11:27.670 --> 01:11:30.180
in us as human beings is one of the first things

1785
01:11:30.180 --> 01:11:32.930
that I explore in both my TEDtalk and the book,

1786
01:11:32.930 --> 01:11:37.170
which is that as a society, what has started happen,

1787
01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:40.450
is that these naturally-occurring experiences

1788
01:11:40.450 --> 01:11:44.270
that really are incredibly important signals to ourselves,

1789
01:11:44.270 --> 01:11:45.590
in terms of how we are doing,

1790
01:11:45.590 --> 01:11:47.650
what's working for us in our lives,

1791
01:11:47.650 --> 01:11:49.010
what's missing in our lives,

1792
01:11:49.010 --> 01:11:53.500
what started to happen is that emotions either feel dirty,

1793
01:11:53.500 --> 01:11:56.850
that they're seen as being disruptive, feminine,

1794
01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:58.730
irrational, illogical,

1795
01:11:58.730 --> 01:12:02.870
or what happens is we receive this narrative from society

1796
01:12:02.870 --> 01:12:05.470
that says to us that there are good emotions

1797
01:12:05.470 --> 01:12:06.960
and bad emotions.

1798
01:12:06.960 --> 01:12:09.480
The good emotions are the joy and the happiness,

1799
01:12:09.480 --> 01:12:10.980
and you should chase happiness,

1800
01:12:10.980 --> 01:12:15.470
and the bad emotions are anger, grief, sadness.

1801
01:12:15.470 --> 01:12:19.410
And so one of the most critical aspects, I think, of my work

1802
01:12:19.410 --> 01:12:23.070
is starting to really challenge this idea

1803
01:12:23.070 --> 01:12:24.527
that they're good or bad emotions.

1804
01:12:24.527 --> 01:12:29.340
And to really put out that our emotions have evolved in us

1805
01:12:29.340 --> 01:12:33.580
as a human species to help us to respond and survive.

1806
01:12:33.580 --> 01:12:35.870
And when we start getting into the space

1807
01:12:35.870 --> 01:12:40.870
where we either block or suppress or push aside emotions,

1808
01:12:41.960 --> 01:12:43.320
we actually stop ourselves

1809
01:12:43.320 --> 01:12:46.840
from being our most effective, successful beings.

1810
01:12:46.840 --> 01:12:49.140
Yeah, this idea that there's a duality

1811
01:12:49.140 --> 01:12:52.780
is a socially-projected notion,

1812
01:12:52.780 --> 01:12:57.780
that perpetuates that vicious cycle of unhealth, I guess,

1813
01:12:58.940 --> 01:13:03.390
because if you feel sad and then, you know,

1814
01:13:03.390 --> 01:13:04.800
well, that's a bad emotion,

1815
01:13:04.800 --> 01:13:07.660
then you're going to then feel shame or guilt

1816
01:13:07.660 --> 01:13:08.493
for having that.

1817
01:13:08.493 --> 01:13:11.060
And you're just digging that hole even deeper and deeper.

1818
01:13:11.060 --> 01:13:12.680
Yeah, that's exactly...

1819
01:13:12.680 --> 01:13:13.930
So it's this fascinating thing

1820
01:13:13.930 --> 01:13:15.940
where we have in psychology,

1821
01:13:15.940 --> 01:13:18.490
we sometimes talk about Type One and Type Two,

1822
01:13:18.490 --> 01:13:23.000
and Type One is where you start saying, "I feel sad."

1823
01:13:23.000 --> 01:13:26.300
And Type Two is when you start having an emotion or judgment

1824
01:13:26.300 --> 01:13:27.230
about the emotion.

1825
01:13:27.230 --> 01:13:29.550
So you say, "I'm sad that I'm sad.

1826
01:13:29.550 --> 01:13:30.383
I shouldn't be sad.

1827
01:13:30.383 --> 01:13:31.216
I push it aside."

1828
01:13:31.216 --> 01:13:33.480
And in some of my work, I, for example,

1829
01:13:33.480 --> 01:13:35.190
did a survey of 70,000 people.

1830
01:13:35.190 --> 01:13:37.430
And I found on that a third of us,

1831
01:13:37.430 --> 01:13:38.820
which is a remarkable number,

1832
01:13:38.820 --> 01:13:41.880
a third of us treat our normal emotions,

1833
01:13:41.880 --> 01:13:45.990
emotions like sadness or anger, or even grief,

1834
01:13:45.990 --> 01:13:48.480
as being bad and so we push them aside.

1835
01:13:48.480 --> 01:13:50.190
Or if we don't do it to ourselves,

1836
01:13:50.190 --> 01:13:52.530
we often do to people we love.

1837
01:13:52.530 --> 01:13:53.363
Our children.

1838
01:13:53.363 --> 01:13:55.223
We jump to a solution.

1839
01:13:56.220 --> 01:13:59.740
And I think a critical aspect of wellbeing

1840
01:13:59.740 --> 01:14:04.740
is moving beyond the struggle with our emotions,

1841
01:14:05.330 --> 01:14:08.397
into the other space, which is,

1842
01:14:08.397 --> 01:14:10.170
"This is how I'm feeling.

1843
01:14:10.170 --> 01:14:14.420
What do I need to do about this context that I'm in."

1844
01:14:14.420 --> 01:14:17.690
Right, to detach from the self-judgment

1845
01:14:17.690 --> 01:14:19.630
that usually is accompanying that?

1846
01:14:19.630 --> 01:14:24.380
Yeah, the radical acceptance of all of our emotions.

1847
01:14:24.380 --> 01:14:26.500
Our grief, our sadness, our anger

1848
01:14:26.500 --> 01:14:30.790
is a hallmark and a cornerstone of resilience

1849
01:14:30.790 --> 01:14:33.050
and a cornerstone of effective relationships.

1850
01:14:33.050 --> 01:14:36.320
That's not to say that because we feel angry,

1851
01:14:36.320 --> 01:14:39.380
we have a right to be angry and we should act on our anger,

1852
01:14:39.380 --> 01:14:43.590
or because we feel wronged, we have been wronged,

1853
01:14:43.590 --> 01:14:45.860
but rather, what's at the core of my work,

1854
01:14:45.860 --> 01:14:50.850
is this idea that our emotions contain signposts

1855
01:14:50.850 --> 01:14:52.720
to things that we care about.

1856
01:14:52.720 --> 01:14:54.110
They're these flashing lights.

1857
01:14:54.110 --> 01:14:57.010
You know, if I feel guilty as a parent,

1858
01:14:57.010 --> 01:15:00.320
it doesn't mean that I should be guilty,

1859
01:15:00.320 --> 01:15:02.760
but it does mean that there's a value often

1860
01:15:02.760 --> 01:15:04.120
that sits beneath that emotion,

1861
01:15:04.120 --> 01:15:08.020
that I value presence and connectedness with my children,

1862
01:15:08.020 --> 01:15:09.610
and that I'm not feeling enough of it.

1863
01:15:09.610 --> 01:15:11.550
I'm not experiencing enough of it.

1864
01:15:11.550 --> 01:15:14.420
So instead of judging the emotion,

1865
01:15:14.420 --> 01:15:18.700
if we can rather be open-hearted and accepting

1866
01:15:18.700 --> 01:15:20.750
and compassionate of it,

1867
01:15:20.750 --> 01:15:22.380
we can start moving into the space

1868
01:15:22.380 --> 01:15:26.050
where we are able to discern values that are underneath it.

1869
01:15:26.050 --> 01:15:29.530
Right, I like the idea that the emotion really sheds light

1870
01:15:29.530 --> 01:15:32.440
on the extent to which you're invested in that value.

1871
01:15:32.440 --> 01:15:36.300
So that guilt reaction really just reaffirms to you

1872
01:15:36.300 --> 01:15:40.010
that that impulse to be a good parent

1873
01:15:40.010 --> 01:15:42.220
is valuable to you, right?
Yes.

1874
01:15:42.220 --> 01:15:46.090
And that's an affirming way to perceive

1875
01:15:46.090 --> 01:15:48.860
what you would ordinarily feel lousy about.

1876
01:15:48.860 --> 01:15:50.818
So again, in the book,

1877
01:15:50.818 --> 01:15:52.390
what do is I talk about this four movements.

1878
01:15:52.390 --> 01:15:53.440
I talk about showing up,

1879
01:15:53.440 --> 01:15:55.410
which is noticing your thoughts and your emotions

1880
01:15:55.410 --> 01:15:56.610
in compassionate ways,

1881
01:15:56.610 --> 01:15:58.900
stepping out, which is creating this space,

1882
01:15:58.900 --> 01:15:59.740
walking your why,

1883
01:15:59.740 --> 01:16:02.290
which is this how do we make values align choices,

1884
01:16:02.290 --> 01:16:03.730
and then, moving on.

1885
01:16:03.730 --> 01:16:04.950
This is this tiny tweaks,

1886
01:16:04.950 --> 01:16:06.780
as well as this teeter totter principle.

1887
01:16:06.780 --> 01:16:11.367
So the idea behind this is that often when in life,

1888
01:16:13.890 --> 01:16:16.630
whether it's in relationships or at work,

1889
01:16:16.630 --> 01:16:20.810
we develop strong levels of overcompetence.

1890
01:16:20.810 --> 01:16:21.750
So the idea here

1891
01:16:21.750 --> 01:16:24.250
is that you can do your job with your eyes closed,

1892
01:16:24.250 --> 01:16:25.480
or you know what to expect.

1893
01:16:25.480 --> 01:16:26.920
And this doesn't mean you aren't busy.

1894
01:16:26.920 --> 01:16:30.350
You might be very busy doing something in a rote way.

1895
01:16:30.350 --> 01:16:32.460
And when we overcompetent,

1896
01:16:32.460 --> 01:16:34.160
it's a very strong risk factor

1897
01:16:34.160 --> 01:16:38.993
for just feelings of disengagement and ultimately,

1898
01:16:39.990 --> 01:16:41.373
a sense of disempowerment.

1899
01:16:42.440 --> 01:16:45.190
So overcompetence is very difficult for us,

1900
01:16:45.190 --> 01:16:49.340
but by the same token, human beings like comfort.

1901
01:16:49.340 --> 01:16:51.870
And we really struggle with the opposite,

1902
01:16:51.870 --> 01:16:53.977
which is overchallenge.

1903
01:16:53.977 --> 01:16:55.070
Overchallenge in a job

1904
01:16:55.070 --> 01:16:56.220
is where you keep feeling

1905
01:16:56.220 --> 01:16:57.730
like you're being thrown in the deep end.

1906
01:16:57.730 --> 01:16:58.880
You never know what's going on.

1907
01:16:58.880 --> 01:17:00.380
The goals keep on changing.

1908
01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:04.000
It's again, a very strong risk factor for disengagement.

1909
01:17:04.000 --> 01:17:07.910
And so the sweet spot of growth in our lives

1910
01:17:07.910 --> 01:17:11.900
is where we neither overcompetent nor overchallenged.

1911
01:17:11.900 --> 01:17:12.733
So what we are doing

1912
01:17:12.733 --> 01:17:17.190
is we are working at the edge of our ability.

1913
01:17:17.190 --> 01:17:20.850
So keeping on again, pushing the boundary,

1914
01:17:20.850 --> 01:17:24.400
not just for the sake of it, again, in a values-aligned way.

1915
01:17:24.400 --> 01:17:25.590
You can take that same idea

1916
01:17:25.590 --> 01:17:27.860
and you can apply it in relationships.

1917
01:17:27.860 --> 01:17:29.190
Where you in a relationship,

1918
01:17:29.190 --> 01:17:34.190
where you go out with your spouse, you go to a movie,

1919
01:17:34.390 --> 01:17:36.240
you know what the person's opinion is of the movie.

1920
01:17:36.240 --> 01:17:37.440
You know what they're gonna order at dinner.

1921
01:17:37.440 --> 01:17:39.190
You know what you're gonna talk about at dinner.

1922
01:17:39.190 --> 01:17:41.130
You overcompetent in that relationship,

1923
01:17:41.130 --> 01:17:43.600
and it's a risk factor for that relationship.

1924
01:17:43.600 --> 01:17:45.155
You also don't wanna be overchallenged,

1925
01:17:45.155 --> 01:17:46.760
where you walking on eggshells.

1926
01:17:46.760 --> 01:17:50.420
So what do we do when are trying to work

1927
01:17:50.420 --> 01:17:51.370
at the edge of our ability?

1928
01:17:51.370 --> 01:17:52.440
Usually what we are tryna do

1929
01:17:52.440 --> 01:17:57.440
is we are tryna either expand breadth or depth.

1930
01:17:57.870 --> 01:18:01.840
Breadth might be we trying new things.

1931
01:18:01.840 --> 01:18:05.230
We moving into environments that are maybe new.

1932
01:18:05.230 --> 01:18:06.676
We may be,

1933
01:18:06.676 --> 01:18:09.570
instead of going out with the same group of friends

1934
01:18:09.570 --> 01:18:11.780
with our spouse every week or the same movie,

1935
01:18:11.780 --> 01:18:13.020
we're trying different things.

1936
01:18:13.020 --> 01:18:14.380
So that's breadth.

1937
01:18:14.380 --> 01:18:16.680
Depth is where you start going deeper,

1938
01:18:16.680 --> 01:18:20.690
where you start developing greater levels of expertise,

1939
01:18:20.690 --> 01:18:21.523
or with your spouse,

1940
01:18:21.523 --> 01:18:23.000
you start having conversations

1941
01:18:23.000 --> 01:18:25.970
that you might not have had for the past 20 years.

1942
01:18:25.970 --> 01:18:27.910
You know, when you actually ask the person

1943
01:18:27.910 --> 01:18:31.470
what their dreams were or what their fears were, or...

1944
01:18:31.470 --> 01:18:34.010
So depth and breadth are usually ways

1945
01:18:34.010 --> 01:18:37.080
that we start expanding the edge,

1946
01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:38.510
and moving at the edge of our ability.

1947
01:18:38.510 --> 01:18:39.870
And it's in that zone

1948
01:18:39.870 --> 01:18:42.560
